# Constraints – Complete Example

The following example uses several constraints together.

```sql
CREATE TABLE student (
    Roll_No INT PRIMARY KEY,
    Name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    Email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE,
    Age INT CHECK (Age >= 18),
    City VARCHAR(30) DEFAULT 'Kakinada'
);
```

### Explanation

| Column    | Constraint    | Purpose                                  |
| --------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `Roll_No` | `PRIMARY KEY` | Uniquely identifies each student         |
| `Name`    | `NOT NULL`    | Name cannot be `NULL`                    |
| `Email`   | `UNIQUE`      | Duplicate email values are not allowed   |
| `Age`     | `CHECK`       | Age must be 18 or above                  |
| `City`    | `DEFAULT`     | Uses `Kakinada` when no city is provided |

---

# Constraint Comparison

| Constraint    | Main Purpose                               |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `NOT NULL`    | Prevents `NULL` values                     |
| `UNIQUE`      | Prevents duplicate values                  |
| `PRIMARY KEY` | Uniquely identifies each row               |
| `FOREIGN KEY` | Creates a relationship between tables      |
| `CHECK`       | Validates values using a condition         |
| `DEFAULT`     | Provides a value when no value is supplied |

---

# Important Differences

## PRIMARY KEY vs UNIQUE

| PRIMARY KEY                    | UNIQUE                                       |
| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| Uniquely identifies each row   | Prevents duplicate values                    |
| Cannot contain `NULL`          | `NULL` handling differs from primary keys    |
| Only one primary key per table | A table can have multiple UNIQUE constraints |
| Can contain multiple columns   | Can also be defined on multiple columns      |

---

## PRIMARY KEY vs FOREIGN KEY

| PRIMARY KEY                          | FOREIGN KEY                                            |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| Identifies a row in its own table    | Refers to a key in another table                       |
| Must be unique                       | Does not have to be unique                             |
| Cannot contain `NULL`                | Can contain `NULL` unless `NOT NULL` is also specified |
| One primary key constraint per table | A table can have multiple foreign keys                 |

---

## DELETE vs DROP vs TRUNCATE

These commands are different and students should understand the difference.

| Command    | Purpose                                            |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `DELETE`   | Removes rows from a table                          |
| `TRUNCATE` | Removes all rows while keeping the table structure |
| `DROP`     | Removes the database object itself                 |

### DELETE

```sql
DELETE FROM student;
```

Removes rows from the table.

### TRUNCATE

```sql
TRUNCATE TABLE student;
```

Removes all rows but keeps the table.

### DROP

```sql
DROP TABLE student;
```

Removes the table itself.

